  "plot_summary": "Spider-Man sees a jewelry burglary about to happen and swoops down to stop the crooks, but he doesn't wait until they break in, so they claim harassment since they haven't done anything illegal and begin calling for the police.&nbsp; Jameson's \"Spider-Man Menace\" posters are everywhere, making it look like <i>he's</i> the problem.&nbsp; Leaving a \"surprise\" in JJJ's empty office, he notices a figure fleeing from the police on a nearby roof and swoops down to confront \"the Sandman,\" and Spider-Man soon learns why.&nbsp; But during the brief scuffle, Peter's mask tears, and he has to flee the scene, causing Sandman to call him a coward.&nbsp; Afterward, the Sandman uses his special abilities to easily enter the bank and rob it.\n\nPeter struggles with sewing up his mask, actually saying \"I'm no cotton-pickin' seamstress!&nbsp; But I've got to do it myself!\"&nbsp; While he's sewing, a news report comes on TV, and reveals the Sandman's origin, that Flint Marko escaped from a max security jail, having to hide in an \"atomic devices testing center.\"&nbsp; But a \"nuclear test explosion caught him unawares\" while he was on a beach, and the molecules of his body merged with the sand, \"becoming virtually indestructible.\"&nbsp; Then the TV cuts to live footage of the Sandman coming out of the bank, with the police trying in vain to stop him.&nbsp; Peter is detained by the ever-doting Aunt May, who thinks he has a fever.\n\nNext morning, JJJ enters his office and sits on the \"surprise\" - webbing left there by Spider-Man.&nbsp; Peter tries to ask for an advance, but JJJ chases him out.&nbsp; (During the scene, we're briefly introduced to JJJ's secretary Miss Brant, who at this point is not given a first name.)&nbsp; Peter heads off to school, carrying the umbrella Aunt May made him take.&nbsp; Peter's day does not go well either.&nbsp; Nothing is turning out good.&nbsp; However, the Sandman is still constantly running from the police, who hound him but can't catch him, until he approaches \"that high school over there\" - he decides to enter it.&nbsp; Of <i>course</i>, it's Peter's high school!\n\nWhile the Sandman threatens the Principal, Peter overhears and bursts in to face him as the Amazing Spider-Man!&nbsp; The kids are just eating it up!&nbsp; He moves the fight out of the classroom into the hallway to give the kids the chance to run, then into the gym where he has more room to operate.&nbsp; Spider-Man's arm eventually gets stuck inside of Sandman, who then butts him with his hardened head, then encases him in sand.\n\nSpider-Man maneuvers him down to the basement where he knows there's an industrial-strength vacuum cleaner.&nbsp; Tricking him with an electric drill into becoming sand, he then aims the vacuum at him, sucking him into it.\n\nThe police and JJJ arrive outside, and Spider-Man hands down the canister with the Sandman in it, careful to stay out of their reach, for he can see that JJJ is inciting everyone against him.&nbsp; JJJ finds Peter Parker inside, who hands him a roll of film with pictures of Spider-Man and Sandman.&nbsp; The police see that JJJ has it out for Spider-Man.\n\nBut when Peter returns to the classroom, he's called \"Mr. Bookworm of 1963!\" and Liz wants nothing to do with him, even though she had agreed to go out on a date with him, but not now!&nbsp; Peter momentarily loses his temper with Flash before backing down, knowing he could really hurt him.&nbsp; Flash gloats and turns to Liz:&nbsp; \"Come on, chick!&nbsp; Let's go!\" (<i>Heh</i>)&nbsp; Hollering insults at Peter, even Liz tells him to tone it back, that he \"needn't be <i>that</i> cruel to Peter.\"\n\nOnce the JJJ editorial is published, the whole town is wondering if Spider-Man is just as bad as the Sandman.&nbsp; We're left with Peter even questioning himself.&nbsp; Is he really a crackpot, seeking fame and fortune?&nbsp; Why is he doing it, but he's been given great power, and he must continue being Spider-Man, hoping the world will eventually understand.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "Another solid entry in the Spider-Man magazine.&nbsp; Flint Marko/Sandman is a formidable foe, and it took brains and ingenuity for Peter to beat him.&nbsp; All the while we were given glimpses of Peter's home, work and school life and the way he can brood about the hassles of being Spider-Man, particularly given the editorial beat-down from JJJ.&nbsp; The main plot with Sandman itself probably only warrants a FN (6.0) rating, but the book as a whole is definitely \"upper-mid-grade\" and holds out much hope for this magazine!&nbsp; I also like the way Ditko weaves in the panels of alternate future events that Spider-Man pictures in his head (e.g. him getting arrested and unmasked in front of the police).&nbsp; It's a nice touch.\n\nI've never been a fan of Ditko's work, having missed all of this early stuff when I started reading comics in 1977 (and always hated seeing his name in the credits since I considered his work very poor compared to the others of that day), but his style just plain <i>works</i> for this character, whose quirky movements and body positions really do stand out, making that odd art style of his seemingly perfect for this magazine.&nbsp; It doesn't bother me at all here, like it did when I was reading books like Machine Man in the late 1970s.&nbsp; The gravelly way he draws the Sandman's face is simply <i>excellent</i>, and the last panel on page 11 is not to be missed - fantastic job!\n\nHad I been a reader in this early time in Marvel's existence, I would take note of the relatively solid quality that this magazine promises, which stands in stark contrast (at least so far), against the unevenness of the Fantastic Four (which can be good or bad in any given month), and the mostly poor quality of its <i>Journey into Strange Tales of Suspense to Astonish</i> brethren.\n\nAlso note-worthy is the fact that up until this point, <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> had been a bi-monthly book, but starting here, the demand (which they discuss in the editorial blurbs themselves) has caused it to change to monthly.&nbsp; We'll see if that affects the quality.&nbsp; Regardless, I definitely find myself looking forward to this book on my reading list and am thankful that will now happen more often!\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n(Reviewer Note:&nbsp; this was the first issue I assigned a \"grade\" during my original pass through the Silver Age Marvels.&nbsp; I've obviously retroactively gone back and done so to previous issues, but it all started with <i>this</i> one.)",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}